    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hi, I'm studying for my phonetics exam and I'm a bit confused. Can you explain the difference between voiced and unvoiced alveolar fricatives? Like, what actually happens with the vocal cords for the /z/ and /s/ sounds?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In phonetics, the alveolar fricatives /z/ and /s/ are distinguished by the voicelessness of the sound. When articulating the /z/ sound, the vocal cords are in a more relaxed position, allowing the airflow to pass through but not vibrating the cords. This results in a voiced sound, often transcribed as \"z\". In contrast, the /s/ sound is produced with the vocal cords in a stiffer position, preventing them from vibrating and creating an unvoiced, hissing sound, often transcribed as \"s\". The specific difference often comes down to the position and tension of the tongue and lips relative to the alveolar ridge.",
      "source": "target",
